          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid retrieval strategies.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - pattern/HybridRetrieval\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Hybrid retrieval pattern from a specialized source; single corroboration point for production prevalence claims.\\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets\u2014often via reciprocal rank fusion\u2014to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### When Hybrid Retrieval Excels\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either lexical or vector search alone on queries that mix:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- Natural language intent\\n\\nThis makes it particularly effective for production RAG systems where query types are heterogeneous.\\n\\n### Implementation\\n\\nThe typical flow:\\n1. Execute a sparse lexical search (BM25 or similar inverted-index method)\\n2. Execute a dense vector search (embedding-based similarity)\\n3. Merge and re-rank results using reciprocal rank fusion or a similar fusion strategy\\n\\n### Status\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-01\\n\u0022}"
        }
